    "nordicscore": {"title":"Jóhann_Jóhannsson","kind":"artist","why":"Reykjavík 2015 is the string-cluster school's arrival on the prestige screen, and no genre article for it exists in this ZIM — the row links the Reykjavík composer whose article is the school's own obituary-shaped summary (drones, tape, a chamber section processed until the room is the instrument), the artist precedent again."},
    "dramascore": {"title":"Thomas_Newman","kind":"artist","why":"Los Angeles 1999 is the suburban-interiors underscore — mallets, felt piano, air — and the school has no genre article in this ZIM, so the row links the composer whose 1999 lawn-and-dusk score every mallet cue since has quoted; his article opens on exactly the scoring career the anchor describes."},
    "frontierscore": {"title":"Jerome_Moross","kind":"artist","why":"Los Angeles 1958 is the year the big-sky main-title grammar was set, and the composer who set it is the honest subject this ZIM holds (`Western music` disambiguates to a hemisphere, the western film article is a picture, not a music) — the horrorscore precedent: the man's article is about the scores, and its own lead names the 1958 main title the label dates."},
    "waltz": {"title":"Waltz","kind":"genre","why":"Vienna 1867 is the Blue Danube's premiere (the piece's own article dates it to the 15th of February 1867 at a Wiener Männergesang-Verein concert), but the anchor is the GENRE, not one piece — `Viennese waltz` in this ZIM is a ballroom-dance article ('a form of ballroom dance'), so the music links the parent form, whose own lead carries the triple time this row finally counts."},
    "musette": {"title":"Bal-musette","kind":"genre","why":"Paris 1880 is the article's own dating — 'first became popular in Paris in the 1880s' — and its lead carries both facts the row is built on: the bagpipe rooms, and the accordion that replaced them. Émile Vacher and Charles Péguri are the players it names for the era."},
    "tarab": {"title":"Tarab","kind":"broader","why":"Cairo 1934 is Umm Kulthum on the Egyptian Radio's inaugural broadcast (her own article's sentence). The article is the CONCEPT — the listening culture whose feedback loop the row's cannot admits it cannot close — 'applied to a style of music and musical performance in which such emotional states are evoked', which is wider than one singer and narrower than Arabic music. Not Umm Kulthum: firqa's row already ruled the singer is not the music. Not Arabic maqam, which is the theory this row's ALPHABET cites."},
